Simulation is not appropriate when:

- the problem can be solved by common sense;
- the problem can be solved analytically;
- it is easier and feasible to perform direct experiments;
- the budget is not sufficient;
- resources or time are not available;
- the system behaviour is too complex;
- no data or estimates are available about the system.

**Simulation** is a technique for studying real-world dynamic systems by imitating their behaviour using a mathematical model of the system implemented on a digital computer.

**Study via simulation is appropriate when we want to:**
- study the interaction of an internal subsystem with the complex system;
- study the effect of informational, organisational and environmental changes;
- gain knowledge about improvement of the system;
- find the important input parameters;
- test new designs and policies before implementation;
- visualise a plan for a system with animated simulation;
- verify analytical solutions.
